<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laguna Copperplate Inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">900</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Laguna Copperplate Inscription is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plate found in 1989 near Lumban in Laguna province, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Dated to 900 CE, it is written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Kawi</a> script in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-malay">Old Malay</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Old Javanese words, and records the cancellation of a debt. As the oldest known document from the Philippines, it shows links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and mentions a leader of Tundun, usually identified with Tondo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laguna-copperplate-inscription.jpg" alt="Laguna Copperplate Inscription" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chipmunkdavis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laguna_Copperplate_Inscription_in_the_National_Museum_of_Anthropology.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
